# d4-06 — workflow integration: rollout, change, cost and cross-team design
team-adoption|4.5|2|A team is rolling a new workflow out to sixty people and asks how they should start.|Start with a small group and learn from what they hit|Roll it out to everyone at once for consistency|Roll it out to the most enthusiastic people first|Roll it out department by department alphabetically|A small first group surfaces the problems while they are still cheap to fix, and what they learn shapes the wider rollout.|An all-at-once launch multiplies every unfixed problem, enthusiasts hide friction others will hit, and an arbitrary order learns nothing between waves.
repeatability|4.3|2|A team changes a workflow's prompt and wants to be able to go back if it goes wrong.|Keep the previous version before making the change|Note what was changed so it can be typed back later|Test the change carefully so a rollback is not needed|Change it gradually so the effect can be observed|A kept copy is what makes a rollback immediate, where a description of the change has to be reconstructed under pressure.|Notes are lossy and slow to apply, careful testing reduces the chance rather than the need, and gradual change still leaves nothing to return to.
failure-modes|4.4|2|An associate is asked what could go wrong with a workflow nobody has tested using bad input.|They cannot say, because it has not been tested|Nothing, since the workflow handles its inputs well|The same things that go wrong with good input|Only the failures that the designer thought about|Untested behaviour is unknown behaviour, and saying so is more useful than an estimate that sounds like knowledge.|Success on good input says nothing about bad, failure modes differ by input, and the designer's list is the hypothesis rather than the answer.
measuring-value|4.2|2|A team wants to measure their workflow and can only pick one single thing to track.|The outcome that the process exists to produce|The number of times the workflow is run each week|The satisfaction of the people who operate it|The time the workflow itself takes to complete|A single measure should be the thing the process is for, since everything else is only a means to it.|Run count measures activity, operator satisfaction measures experience, and runtime measures one step rather than the result.
handoffs|4.4|2|A workflow's output goes to three different teams who each need something different from it.|Ask each team what they need before designing it|Produce one output covering everything all three need|Produce the output that the largest of the teams needs|Let each team extract what they need from the output|Three consumers means three requirements, and collecting them is what lets the output be designed rather than guessed.|A union of everything buries each team's part, sizing by team ignores need, and pushing extraction downstream repeats the work three times.
tool-integration|4.4|2|A team's workflow reads from a system whose owners are about to change its data format.|Agree a date and test against the new format first|Wait until the change happens and fix what breaks|Ask the owners to delay the change until later on|Copy the data out now so the format stops mattering|A known upcoming change is something to prepare for, and testing before the switch is what turns a breakage into a non-event.|Fixing after the break accepts an outage, asking for a delay postpones rather than solves, and a copy goes stale immediately.
automation-boundaries|4.4|2|A team is asked whether a task that is performed twice a year is worth automating.|Usually not — the build effort will not be repaid|Yes, since automation is always worth the investment|Yes, since twice a year is still a recurring task|No, since infrequent tasks are always done manually|Automation pays back through repetition, and twice a year rarely returns the cost of building and maintaining it.|"Always worth it" ignores maintenance, recurrence alone does not settle the arithmetic, and an absolute rule ignores high-value exceptions.
process-mapping|4.1|2|An associate is told a process takes two days and finds that it is two days of waiting.|Distinguish the working time from the waiting time|Report the two days as the process's duration|Reduce the working time to shorten the two days|Ask the team to work faster during the two days|Waiting and working are different problems with different fixes, and separating them is what shows where the time actually goes.|Reporting the total hides the structure, and speeding up work that is not the constraint leaves the waiting exactly where it was.
human-in-the-loop|4.4|2|A team asks what makes a review step a real control rather than a formality.|The reviewer can and sometimes does reject things|The reviewer is senior enough to be accountable|The review is recorded so it can be audited later|The review happens before the output is released|A control is real when it changes outcomes, and rejections are the evidence that it can.|Seniority, records and timing all describe the shape of a review that may still never change anything.
failure-modes|4.4|2|A team's workflow fails and nobody can tell which step it actually failed at.|Record which step each run reached before stopping|Rerun the workflow and watch it as it runs through|Add more detail to the workflow's final output|Ask whoever ran it what they saw at the time it failed|Locating a failure requires a record of progress, and capturing the step reached turns every future failure into a diagnosis.|A watched rerun may not reproduce it, output detail describes success rather than failure, and recollection is not a record.
measuring-value|4.2|2|A team is asked for their workflow's cost and gives only the software licence fee.|Include the time spent building and maintaining it|Give the licence fee, since that is the direct cost|Include the cost of the systems it connects to|Include the salary of everyone who uses the workflow|The real cost of a workflow is dominated by the human effort to build and keep it running, which the licence line never shows.|The licence is the visible fraction, connected systems exist regardless, and full salaries charge the workflow for work people would do anyway.
team-adoption|4.5|2|A team's workflow requires each person to complete a setup step, and half of them have not.|Find out what makes the setup step hard to complete|Send a reminder asking people to complete the setup|Complete the setup on everyone's behalf centrally|Remove the feature that requires the setup step|A step half the team has not done is usually harder or less clear than it looks, and finding out is what makes it completable.|A reminder repeats an instruction that has not worked, doing it centrally may be impossible for a personal setting, and dropping the feature discards its value.
handoffs|4.4|2|A workflow's output arrives with the receiving person while they are away on leave.|Route the output to a role rather than to a person|Ask the person to check their messages while away|Hold the output until the person returns to work|Send the output to the whole team instead of one person|Routing to a role means the handoff survives any individual's absence without anyone having to remember to redirect it.|Expecting work during leave is not a design, holding output stalls the process, and broadcasting to everyone means nobody owns it.
automation-boundaries|4.4|3|A team automated a step and the person who used to do it now checks the automation instead.|Ask whether the checking costs less than the step did|Accept it, since a person is still involved in the step|Remove the checking, since the automation is working|Automate the checking as well so the person is freed|If checking costs as much as doing, the automation has moved the work rather than reduced it, and comparing the two shows which happened.|Human involvement is not the goal, removing the check before its value is known is premature, and automating a check produces something else to check.
repeatability|4.3|3|A team has four similar workflows built by four different people and wants to consolidate them.|Find what the four actually have in common first|Pick the best of the four and have everyone use it|Build a fifth workflow that covers all four cases|Keep all four, since each was built for a reason|Four workflows may share most of their substance or almost none, and knowing which decides whether consolidation is possible at all.|Picking one imposes its assumptions on three other jobs, a fifth adds to the sprawl before understanding it, and keeping all four accepts the duplication.
measuring-value|4.2|3|A team's workflow is measured on throughput and the team has started routing easy work to it.|Change the measure to something that cannot be gamed|Tell the team to stop routing the work selectively|Accept it, since throughput has genuinely increased|Measure the difficulty of the work as well as the volume|A measure that rewards selection will be satisfied by selection, so the fix is a measure whose only route to improvement is the real one.|Instructing people not to respond to an incentive rarely holds, the increase is an artefact, and a second measure leaves the first one still pulling.
team-adoption|4.5|3|A team's workflow is adopted quickly and people use it for a purpose it was not designed for.|Look at whether the new use is better than the old one|Redirect people back to the originally intended purpose|Block the unintended use so the original design holds|Rebuild the workflow around the unintended use|Users finding a better use is information, and evaluating it is what decides whether the design or the usage should change.|Redirecting and blocking both assume the original design was right, and rebuilding assumes the opposite without checking.
failure-modes|4.4|3|A team's workflow has run for a year without failing and they are asked for their recovery plan.|Write one, since a year of success is not a plan|Say the workflow has not needed one in a year|Write one only if the workflow starts failing|Rely on rebuilding it from scratch if it ever fails|A recovery plan is written while nothing is broken, because the moment it is needed is the worst possible moment to design one.|A clean record is not a control, waiting for failure defers the work to the crisis, and rebuilding from scratch is a plan only if it has been costed.
tool-integration|4.4|3|An associate's integration works and the team wants a second one built the same way.|Check whether the first one's choices suit the second|Copy the first integration and change what differs|Build the second from scratch to avoid shared problems|Make the first one generic enough to serve both|What made the first integration right may not hold for the second, and checking prevents an assumption being copied along with the design.|Copying carries the assumptions silently, building from scratch discards what was learned, and generalising early designs for a case not yet understood.
handoffs|4.4|3|A workflow's two halves are owned by different teams and neither knows the other's constraints.|Have each side state what it needs from the other|Move both halves into a single team's ownership|Have one team document their half for the other|Add a coordinator sitting between the two teams|A working interface needs both sides' requirements on the table, and stating them is the cheapest way to get there.|Reorganising is a heavy answer to a communication gap, one-sided documentation leaves the other side unstated, and a coordinator relays rather than resolves.
process-mapping|4.1|3|A team is told to speed up a process and finds the slowest step is also the most valuable.|Look for time elsewhere before touching that step|Speed up the valuable step, since it is the slowest|Leave the process alone, since the value justifies it|Split the valuable step so parts of it run in parallel|Cutting time from the step that carries the value risks the outcome, so the cheaper time is found in the steps that carry less.|Targeting the slowest step ignores what it produces, refusing to change anything abandons the goal, and parallelising is a fix chosen before the search.
human-in-the-loop|4.4|3|A team's reviewers see the output but never the input the workflow worked from.|Give the reviewers the input alongside the output|Ask reviewers to request the input when they need it|Have the workflow summarise its input in the output|Train reviewers to judge outputs on their own terms|Judging whether an output is right requires knowing what it was meant to be about, which is what the input provides.|On-demand requests slow every review, a generated summary inherits the output's errors, and judging output alone checks style rather than correctness.
repeatability|4.3|3|A team's workflow behaves differently for two people because of their personal settings.|Move the settings that matter into the workflow itself|Ask both people to align their personal settings|Have one of the two people run everything instead|Document the settings each person should be using|A workflow that depends on personal configuration is not a shared workflow, and pulling the relevant settings into it removes the dependency.|Manual alignment drifts, a single operator is a bottleneck, and documentation relies on everyone applying it correctly.
automation-boundaries|4.4|3|A team wants to automate a judgement that two experienced staff make differently from each other.|Settle the judgement between them before automating|Automate whichever version is applied more often|Automate both and let the workflow pick one of them|Leave it manual, since the experts genuinely disagree|You cannot automate a rule that has not been agreed, so the disagreement is the thing to resolve first.|Frequency does not make a judgement right, encoding both defers the disagreement into the workflow, and leaving it manual preserves the inconsistency.
measuring-value|4.2|3|A team's workflow benefit is that a rare and expensive mistake now happens less often.|Report the expected cost avoided, and say it is expected|Report the number of mistakes since the workflow began|Wait until enough time has passed to show a difference|Report the benefit as unmeasurable in the short term|A rare-event benefit is estimated rather than counted, and presenting it as an expectation is both usable and honest.|Counting rare events over a short window shows nothing, waiting delays a decision indefinitely, and calling it unmeasurable understates a real benefit.
tool-integration|4.4|3|A team's workflow calls a service that charges per request and the volume is growing.|Check whether requests are being made unnecessarily|Negotiate a better rate with the service provider|Cap the number of requests the workflow may make|Move to a cheaper service that does the same thing|Growing volume often includes repeated or avoidable calls, and finding those reduces the bill without changing anything else.|Negotiating and switching both address the price rather than the usage, and a cap limits the workflow rather than its waste.
team-adoption|4.5|3|A team's workflow saves each person ten minutes a day and nobody has changed how they work.|Ask what the ten minutes would need to be for|Accept it, since the time saving is real regardless|Report the saving as ten minutes times the headcount|Add more to the workflow until the saving is noticeable|Time saved in small slices often disappears rather than being redirected, and asking what it could enable is what turns it into value.|An unrealised saving is not a benefit, multiplying it up asserts value that has not appeared, and expanding scope assumes the saving is the problem.
process-mapping|4.1|4|A team maps a process, finds every step necessary, and the total time is still too long.|Look at what happens between the steps rather than in them|Accept the duration, since every step has been justified|Remove the least valuable step despite its necessity|Run several of the necessary steps at the same time|When the steps themselves are all justified, the remaining time is in the gaps between them, which is usually where most of it sits.|Accepting it abandons the goal, removing a necessary step breaks the process, and parallelising is one possible fix chosen before the time has been located.
human-in-the-loop|4.4|4|A team's approval step is staffed by someone who cannot evaluate the content they approve.|Move the approval to someone who can evaluate it|Train the current approver to evaluate the content|Give the approver a checklist to work through instead|Remove the step, since the approval means nothing|An approval by someone who cannot judge the content is a signature rather than a control, and the fix is to put it where the judgement exists.|Training is slower than reassignment and may not be feasible, a checklist substitutes procedure for judgement, and removing it loses a control that should exist.
failure-modes|4.4|4|A team's workflow can fail loudly and stop, or fail quietly and continue with degraded output.|Choose by whether a wrong result or a stopped process costs more|Always fail loudly, since silent failures are dangerous|Always continue, since stopping disrupts everyone downstream|Fail loudly in testing and continue quietly in production|Both behaviours are defensible and the right one depends on what the process can absorb, so the comparison of costs is the decision.|Absolute rules in either direction ignore the process, and behaving differently in production is exactly where the untested path lives.
measuring-value|4.2|4|A team's workflow moved work from an expensive team to a cheaper one and the total time rose.|Ask whether the cost saving is worth the added delay|Report it as a saving, since the cost has come down|Report it as a failure, since the process got slower|Move the work back, since the change made things worse|Cost and speed have moved in opposite directions, so the question is which one the process is actually optimising for.|Reporting either figure alone hides the other, and reverting decides the trade-off without anyone having stated it.
handoffs|4.4|4|A workflow passes a summary to a decision-maker, and it is accurate but omits an exception.|Have the summary name what it has deliberately left out|Include the exceptions so that nothing at all is omitted|Have the decision-maker read the source document instead|Accept it, since a summary has to leave things out|A summary that states its own boundaries lets the reader know when to go further, which is what makes summarising safe.|Including everything stops it being a summary, sending them to the source removes its value, and accepting silent omission is the failure being described.
automation-boundaries|4.4|4|A team can automate a step, and doing so would leave nobody who understands how it works.|Automate it and keep a written account of the reasoning|Automate it, since understanding is not needed to run it|Leave it manual so the understanding stays in the team|Automate it and rotate people through the manual version|The understanding is what allows the step to be changed or fixed later, and writing it down preserves it without paying for the manual work forever.|Understanding matters the moment something breaks, keeping the step manual is an expensive way to store knowledge, and rotation maintains a process solely as training.
repeatability|4.3|4|A team's workflow gives the same answer every time and that answer has been wrong for months.|Consistency was never evidence that it was correct|The consistency shows the workflow is working properly|The error must therefore have been introduced recently|Repeatability and correctness are really the same property|A workflow reproduces whatever it does, including a mistake, so stability tells you about the process rather than about the answer.|Treating consistency as correctness is the misconception, recency is an assumption, and conflating the two properties is the same error stated directly.
tool-integration|4.4|4|An associate must decide whether the workflow should hold its own copy of reference data.|Copy it where staleness costs less than an outage would|Always read it live, since a copy will go out of date|Always copy it, since the workflow then has no dependency|Copy it and refresh the copy on a fixed schedule|A local copy trades freshness for independence, and which of those the workflow can better afford is what decides.|Absolute rules ignore the trade, and a fixed refresh sets the staleness window before anyone has said what it should be.
team-adoption|4.5|4|A team's workflow is genuinely better and its users feel it makes their job smaller.|Take the concern seriously as part of the design|Explain that the workflow frees them for better work|Proceed, since the improvement is objectively real|Pause the rollout until people come to feel differently|How a change lands on the people doing the work determines whether it survives, which makes it a design input rather than an objection to manage.|Explaining talks past the concern, proceeding on objective merit is how good tools get abandoned, and pausing waits for a feeling to change on its own.
failure-modes|4.4|4|A team's error rate is within the limit they agreed and the errors fall mostly on one customer group.|Look at the distribution, since an accepted rate can land unevenly|Accept it, since the overall rate is within the agreed limit|Reduce the overall rate until that group's share falls|Exclude that customer group from the workflow entirely|An acceptable average can conceal one group bearing most of the harm, which is a different question from whether the rate is low.|The agreed limit was set on the average, lowering the overall rate need not change the distribution, and excluding the group denies them the service.
measuring-value|4.2|4|A team is asked for one number and the costs and benefits are genuinely different in kind.|Give the numbers separately and say why they do not combine|Give a combined figure using the team's best conversion|Give the benefit figure, since that is what was asked about|Decline to give any number until they can be compared|Forcing incomparable things into one number hides the judgement inside it, and showing them separately keeps that judgement with the decision-maker.|A conversion smuggles in an assumption, reporting one side is incomplete, and refusing outright leaves the decision without information.
human-in-the-loop|4.4|4|A team's reviewers approve polished output faster, and errors in polished output are the costly ones.|Have reviewers check against the source rather than reading it|Ask reviewers to be more sceptical of polished output|Have the workflow produce rather less polished output|Add a second reviewer for the most polished outputs|Fluency is what defeats reading-based review, so anchoring the check to the source is what makes polish stop mattering.|Asking for scepticism relies on resisting a strong effect, degrading the output damages it for readers, and a second reader repeats the same vulnerable check.
tool-integration|4.4|4|An integration is the only automated access to a system and the team wants more built on it.|Ask what the system's owners expect before building further|Build on it, since the integration already works reliably|Build a separate integration for each new use case|Stop, since one integration should not carry several uses|Being the only automated route makes it a dependency the system's owners have a stake in, and their expectations shape what can safely be built on it.|Working today is not permission, one integration per use multiplies the dependency, and refusing forgoes legitimate reuse.
